  "why": "Both directions of the macro-bisection blow through the 2x threshold by a wide margin: training on the small 16-polymer macro-chemotype and cold-predicting the other 1061 gives MAE=0.5604 g/cm3 (13.74x within-corpus, R2=-12.15); the reverse (train on the 1061-polymer bulk, cold-predict the 16-polymer outlier macro-chemotype) gives MAE=0.3098 g/cm3 (7.59x within-corpus, R2=-5.01). Both R2 values are strongly negative (worse than predicting the training median in absolute terms once compared to within-corpus skill), and direction A's null-baseline comparison shows the cross-chemotype RF (MAE 0.560) barely beats even the naive train-median null (0.642) -- essentially no extractable skill once the model must extrapolate outside its training macro-chemotype's density range. This directly refutes the 'transfers to out-of-corpus chemistry' claim for density under a genuinely falsifying, chemically-coherent held-out split, closing the gap fire 1 left open (leave-class-out at 1.80x SURVIVED, but was flagged there as not equivalent to a true second-corpus test)."
